We introduce ImPULSeS, a novel framework that enhances the performance of positive and unlabeled learning by integrating a self-reinforcing process with a self-correcting mechanism. This framework utilizes a self-reinforcing process to generate reliable pseudo-negative samples and a self-correcting mechanism to refine the pseudo-positive samples. By leveraging the self-reinforcing process, ImPULSeS significantly outperforms existing positive and unlabeled learning methods, achieving a notable improvement of 20.0% in terms of F1-score. Furthermore, ImPULSeS can be seamlessly integrated with other positive and unlabeled learning methods to build upon existing strengths, resulting in substantial performance enhancements of up to 50.0%. Our framework also showcases its adaptability by successfully learning from datasets with varying characteristics, such as those with limited class imbalance, where ImPULSeS outperforms existing methods by a margin of 17.1%. The code and data used in this research are publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/IBM/ImPULSeS. Additionally, the source code is also accessible in the IBM Research Automated Machine Learning Library. We anticipate that ImPULSeS will serve as a valuable resource for the machine learning community. We also hope that our work will inspire further research in the area of positive and unlabeled learning. We are committed to making our research findings and resources accessible to the public. The source code and data used in this research are available at https://github.com/IBM/ImPULSeS. We also welcome contributions and feedback from the community. The source code is also accessible in the IBM Research Automated Machine Learning Library. We are committed to making our research findings and resources accessible to the public. The source code and data used in this research are available at https://github.com/IBM/ImPULSeS. We also welcome contributions and feedback from the community. We are grateful to the sup