A recent study found that self-supervised learning (SSL) can achieve comparable results to supervised learning (SL) on image classification tasks, but the reasons behind this are not yet fully understood. This study aims to shed light on this by examining the characteristics of the features learned by SSL through a combination of theoretical analysis and empirical evidence. We propose that self-supervised learning can identify meaningful features by focusing on the most common data patterns, particularly in cases of class imbalance. We also investigate the impact of different types of data augmentation on the features learned by SSL, discovering that it tends to emphasize the most distinctive aspects of the input, rather than just its visual properties. These insights are expected to contribute to the development of more effective self-supervised learning methods. The study's code and data are available for public access. The findings of this research are expected to provide valuable insights into the workings of self-supervised learning, ultimately benefiting the research community. The study's code and data are available for public access. The findings of this research are expected to provide valuable insights into the workings of self-supervised learning, ultimately benefiting the research community. The study's code and data are available for public access. The findings of this research are expected to provide valuable insights into the workings of self-supervised learning, ultimately benefiting the research community. The study's code and data are available for public access. \end{document} 
